Okay! Use the following solution for the 401 issue.

Why do you receive 401 Invalid JWT?
	1.	The Edge Function sees that you sent the Authorization:[REDACTED] … header.
	•	Supabase treats any Authorization:[REDACTED] <token> as a user JWT for verification.
	•	You are currently passing in the VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY (API Key), which is not in JWT format, so logically it is considered an Invalid JWT → 401.
	2.	You do not intend to use user authentication.
	•	If you only want the frontend to call a "public" Edge Function to place an order with TapPay, you do not need to include a user JWT at all.
	•	Supabase Edge Functions allow access by default with just an apikey (or x-api-key); including Authorization triggers the verification mechanism.

Solution 1: Remove Authorization, send apikey instead.

// ✅ Keep only apikey
const headers = {
  'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  'apikey': import.meta.env.VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY   // ← anon key
};

- const headers = {
-   'Content-Type': 'application/json',
-   'Authorization': `Bearer ${import.meta.env.VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY}`
- };
+ const headers = {
+   'Content-Type': 'application/json',
+   'apikey': import.meta.env.VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
+ };

Advantages: The most straightforward method, it will immediately work and the Edge Function will treat your request as a "public request."
Note: If in the future you want to read req.headers.get('apikey') for permission verification, remember to read this header within the function.

